Here’s the tight version you can drop into JIRA.

User Story

As a user, when my profile or region changes, the app reads iConfig, resolves the correct report config, and uses it for Run Report requests without me refreshing the page.

Acceptance Criteria
	1.	Initial load

	•	Given the app boots
	•	When iConfig is available
	•	Then the app loads the default report config and exposes it via ReportConfigService.getConfig$()
	•	And the value is ready before the first Run Report call.

	2.	Profile change reacts

	•	Given I change profile
	•	When the profile change event fires
	•	Then the app fetches the matching config from iConfig and updates getConfig$()
	•	And any open report UI rebinds to the new config without a full reload.

	3.	Region change reacts

	•	Given I change region
	•	When the region change event fires
	•	Then the config is refetched for that region and published to subscribers
	•	And subsequent Run Report calls use the new config.

	4.	Request uses current config

	•	Given multiple profile or region flips happen quickly
	•	When a Run Report call goes out
	•	Then the payload uses the latest config at send time (last update wins).

	5.	Caching and freshness

	•	Given the same profile+region is selected again within the cache window
	•	When the service resolves config
	•	Then it serves from cache
	•	And a manual forceRefresh() bypasses cache.

	6.	Fallback and error handling

	•	If iConfig fails (network or 5xx), keep the last known good config and surface a non-blocking warning
	•	If iConfig returns invalid or missing keys, fall back to defaults and log a validation error.

	7.	Validation

	•	Config is schema-validated in the client. Unknown keys are ignored, required keys are present, and types match. Invalid config never reaches the API layer.

	8.	Telemetry

	•	On every load or change, log one event with profile, region, config version, cache hit vs miss, success vs failure.

	9.	Performance

	•	Config resolution completes in under 300 ms on cache hit and under 1 s on cold load in normal network conditions.

	10.	Security and privacy

	•	No secrets from iConfig are logged
	•	PII is not persisted in local storage. Only profile+region identifiers and a small cache entry are stored if needed.

	11.	Testing

	•	Unit tests cover mapping, caching, validation, and change handling
	•	E2E test verifies UI and request payload switch on profile/region change.

Jira Subtasks
	1.	Define types and schema

	•	Create ReportConfig and IConfigReportSchema TypeScript interfaces
	•	Add a runtime validator (Zod or custom) and default values map
	•	Output: report-config.types.ts, report-config.schema.ts

	2.	iConfig client

	•	Build IConfigClient to read keys from iConfig (env aware)
	•	Support getReportConfig(profile, region, { force })
	•	Output: iconfig.client.ts

	3.	ReportConfigService with cache

	•	Implement ReportConfigService using BehaviorSubject
	•	In-memory cache keyed by profile|region with TTL
	•	Expose getConfig$(), load(profile, region), forceRefresh()
	•	Output: report-config.service.ts

	4.	Subscribe to profile and region changes

	•	Wire to UserProfileService and RegionService (or app store)
	•	Debounce/throttle rapid toggles, ensure last update wins
	•	Output: report-context.effects.ts or integration in a shared initializer

	5.	App initializer

	•	Add APP_INITIALIZER to load baseline config on boot using current profile+region
	•	Block Run Report UI until ready state is true
	•	Output: app.initializer.ts and module wiring

	6.	Integrate with RunReportService

	•	Consume ReportConfigService.getConfig$() in the request builder
	•	Ensure payload reflects current config at call time
	•	Add guards to prevent sending if config is invalid
	•	Output: run-report.service.ts update

	7.	UI rebind

	•	Make report screens subscribe to config stream
	•	When config changes, update form defaults, dropdown sources, visibility, and validation
	•	Avoid full component reloads
	•	Output: updates to report components

	8.	Error and empty states

	•	Snackbar or inline banner on fallback
	•	“Try again” triggers forceRefresh()
	•	Output: shared ConfigStatusBannerComponent or simple hook

	9.	Telemetry

	•	Emit events on load, cache hit, cache miss, failure
	•	Add correlation id to Run Report requests with config version
	•	Output: telemetry.events.ts and service calls

	10.	Unit tests

	•	Tests for: schema validation, mapping, cache, last-write-wins, error handling
	•	Aim for ≥80% coverage in the affected files
	•	Output: *.spec.ts

	11.	E2E tests

	•	Mock iConfig with different responses per profile/region
	•	Verify UI updates and Run Report payload content after toggles
	•	Output: Cypress tests

	12.	Docs

	•	Dev notes: config keys, data flow diagram, how to add new keys
	•	Troubleshooting: stale cache, invalid config, missing keys
	•	Output: Confluence page and README.md in the feature folder

	13.	Feature flag and env wiring

	•	Toggle to disable dynamic config if needed
	•	Add iConfig base URL and keys to environment.*.ts
	•	Output: env updates and flag guard

	14.	QA checklist and handoff

	•	Scenarios list, test data, how to simulate failures
	•	Output: checklist in JIRA and attached to the story

